GAD.reg("pyq","n-p6-pyq-may2025",[{"id":"n-p6-pyq-may2025-fm-m1","section":"I","no":1,"marks":2,"type":"MCQ","stem":"What will be the total manufacturing cost and total cost of sales on cash cost basis?","options":["₹ 20,70,000, ₹ 23,40,000","₹ 24,00,000, ₹ 30,00,000","₹ 22,50,000, ₹ 23,40,000","₹ 16,80,000, ₹ 24,00,000"],"group":{"kind":"case","id":"n-p6-pyq-may2025-fm-c1","label":"Case Scenario - I","context":"XYZ Ltd. wants to establish a shoe manufacturing unit. To setup this unit, it needs a loan. XYZ Ltd, approaches a commercial bank for working capital loan.\n\nBank has asked the company to present the proposal for such loan. To prepare such proposal, the company has appointed you and provided some information about the plan.\n\nIt wants to maintain margin of safety of 10% for contingencies. The company want to keep cash balance of ₹ 90,000.\n\nThe product will be sold at gross profit margin of 25% on COGS. Depreciation will be part of cost of production.\n\nStock of raw material will be held at 1.5 months of its consumption, while finished goods inventory will be maintained at one month's requirement. Finished goods will be valued at manufacturing cost.\n\nThe company will sell shoes in domestic as well as in foreign market. Total estimated annual sales will be ₹ 30,00,000. 20% of the sales are foreign sales and there will be a delay of 1/2 month to realise the sales proceed from foreign debtors. All domestic sales will be on credit and allowed a credit period of 2 months.\n\nRaw material consumed will be 25% of estimated sales and the company will get credit period of 2 months of consumption from its supplier of raw material. Wage expenses will be 20% of sales and will be paid in 1/2 month's lag.\n\nOutstanding cash manufacturing overhead expenses at the end of the year will be ₹ 60,000; it will be paid in one month arrear. Estimated total administrative cost (to be paid after one month) will be ₹ 1,80,000. Expected annual sales promotion expenses are ₹ 90,000, which will be paid quarterly in advance. The company want to submit the proposal to bank on cash cost basis.\n\nYou are required to answer the following question 1 to 5:"},"answer":{"kind":"single","value":"A"},"key_raw":"A","label":"MCQ 1","ord":0,"ours":{"approach":"Work back from sales: a 25% margin on COGS makes the whole cost of sales 30,00,000 ÷ 1.25. One month's outstanding overheads give the yearly cash overheads; depreciation is then the balancing figure, and a cash cost basis leaves it out.","difficulty":"medium"},"pg":{"q":"2","a":"7","cq":"1-2"},"case":"n-p6-pyq-may2025-fm-c1","sub":"fm","tags":["fm.wc.estimate"]},{"id":"n-p6-pyq-may2025-fm-m2","section":"I","no":2,"marks":2,"type":"MCQ","stem":"What will be the total estimated finished goods inventory and total debtors on cash cost basis?","options":["₹ 2,00,000, ₹ 4,25,000","₹ 1,72,500, ₹ 3,40,000","₹ 1,72,500, ₹ 3,31,500","₹ 1,87,500, ₹ 3,31,500"],"group":{"kind":"case","id":"n-p6-pyq-may2025-fm-c1","label":"Case Scenario - I","context":"XYZ Ltd. wants to establish a shoe manufacturing unit. To setup this unit, it needs a loan. XYZ Ltd, approaches a commercial bank for working capital loan.\n\nBank has asked the company to present the proposal for such loan. To prepare such proposal, the company has appointed you and provided some information about the plan.\n\nIt wants to maintain margin of safety of 10% for contingencies. The company want to keep cash balance of ₹ 90,000.\n\nThe product will be sold at gross profit margin of 25% on COGS. Depreciation will be part of cost of production.\n\nStock of raw material will be held at 1.5 months of its consumption, while finished goods inventory will be maintained at one month's requirement. Finished goods will be valued at manufacturing cost.\n\nThe company will sell shoes in domestic as well as in foreign market. Total estimated annual sales will be ₹ 30,00,000. 20% of the sales are foreign sales and there will be a delay of 1/2 month to realise the sales proceed from foreign debtors. All domestic sales will be on credit and allowed a credit period of 2 months.\n\nRaw material consumed will be 25% of estimated sales and the company will get credit period of 2 months of consumption from its supplier of raw material. Wage expenses will be 20% of sales and will be paid in 1/2 month's lag.\n\nOutstanding cash manufacturing overhead expenses at the end of the year will be ₹ 60,000; it will be paid in one month arrear. Estimated total administrative cost (to be paid after one month) will be ₹ 1,80,000. Expected annual sales promotion expenses are ₹ 90,000, which will be paid quarterly in advance. The company want to submit the proposal to bank on cash cost basis.\n\nYou are required to answer the following question 1 to 5:"},"answer":{"kind":"single","value":"C"},"key_raw":"C","label":"MCQ 2","ord":1,"ours":{"approach":"Finished goods are one month of cash manufacturing cost; debtors are taken at cash cost of sales, split into the domestic (2 months) and foreign (1/2 month) shares.","difficulty":"medium"},"pg":{"q":"2","a":"7","cq":"1-2"},"case":"n-p6-pyq-may2025-fm-c1","sub":"fm","tags":["fm.wc.estimate"]},{"id":"n-p6-pyq-may2025-fm-m3","section":"I","no":3,"marks":2,"type":"MCQ","stem":"What will be the total estimated current assets on cash cost basis?","options":["₹ 8,31,250","₹ 7,18,750","₹ 7,25,250","₹ 7,10,250"],"group":{"kind":"case","id":"n-p6-pyq-may2025-fm-c1","label":"Case Scenario - I","context":"XYZ Ltd. wants to establish a shoe manufacturing unit. To setup this unit, it needs a loan. XYZ Ltd, approaches a commercial bank for working capital loan.\n\nBank has asked the company to present the proposal for such loan. To prepare such proposal, the company has appointed you and provided some information about the plan.\n\nIt wants to maintain margin of safety of 10% for contingencies. The company want to keep cash balance of ₹ 90,000.\n\nThe product will be sold at gross profit margin of 25% on COGS. Depreciation will be part of cost of production.\n\nStock of raw material will be held at 1.5 months of its consumption, while finished goods inventory will be maintained at one month's requirement. Finished goods will be valued at manufacturing cost.\n\nThe company will sell shoes in domestic as well as in foreign market. Total estimated annual sales will be ₹ 30,00,000. 20% of the sales are foreign sales and there will be a delay of 1/2 month to realise the sales proceed from foreign debtors. All domestic sales will be on credit and allowed a credit period of 2 months.\n\nRaw material consumed will be 25% of estimated sales and the company will get credit period of 2 months of consumption from its supplier of raw material. Wage expenses will be 20% of sales and will be paid in 1/2 month's lag.\n\nOutstanding cash manufacturing overhead expenses at the end of the year will be ₹ 60,000; it will be paid in one month arrear. Estimated total administrative cost (to be paid after one month) will be ₹ 1,80,000. Expected annual sales promotion expenses are ₹ 90,000, which will be paid quarterly in advance. The company want to submit the proposal to bank on cash cost basis.\n\nYou are required to answer the following question 1 to 5:"},"answer":{"kind":"single","value":"D"},"key_raw":"D","label":"MCQ 3","ord":2,"ours":{"approach":"Add raw material stock, finished goods, debtors, the prepaid sales promotion (a quarter paid in advance) and the cash balance.","difficulty":"medium"},"pg":{"q":"2","a":"7","cq":"1-2"},"case":"n-p6-pyq-may2025-fm-c1","sub":"fm","tags":["fm.wc.estimate"]},{"id":"n-p6-pyq-may2025-fm-m4","section":"I","no":4,"marks":2,"type":"MCQ","stem":"What will be the total estimated current liabilities?","options":["₹ 2,52,500","₹ 1,90,000","₹ 2,17,500","₹ 2,25,000"],"group":{"kind":"case","id":"n-p6-pyq-may2025-fm-c1","label":"Case Scenario - I","context":"XYZ Ltd. wants to establish a shoe manufacturing unit. To setup this unit, it needs a loan. XYZ Ltd, approaches a commercial bank for working capital loan.\n\nBank has asked the company to present the proposal for such loan. To prepare such proposal, the company has appointed you and provided some information about the plan.\n\nIt wants to maintain margin of safety of 10% for contingencies. The company want to keep cash balance of ₹ 90,000.\n\nThe product will be sold at gross profit margin of 25% on COGS. Depreciation will be part of cost of production.\n\nStock of raw material will be held at 1.5 months of its consumption, while finished goods inventory will be maintained at one month's requirement. Finished goods will be valued at manufacturing cost.\n\nThe company will sell shoes in domestic as well as in foreign market. Total estimated annual sales will be ₹ 30,00,000. 20% of the sales are foreign sales and there will be a delay of 1/2 month to realise the sales proceed from foreign debtors. All domestic sales will be on credit and allowed a credit period of 2 months.\n\nRaw material consumed will be 25% of estimated sales and the company will get credit period of 2 months of consumption from its supplier of raw material. Wage expenses will be 20% of sales and will be paid in 1/2 month's lag.\n\nOutstanding cash manufacturing overhead expenses at the end of the year will be ₹ 60,000; it will be paid in one month arrear. Estimated total administrative cost (to be paid after one month) will be ₹ 1,80,000. Expected annual sales promotion expenses are ₹ 90,000, which will be paid quarterly in advance. The company want to submit the proposal to bank on cash cost basis.\n\nYou are required to answer the following question 1 to 5:"},"answer":{"kind":"single","value":"D"},"key_raw":"D","label":"MCQ 4","ord":3,"ours":{"approach":"Current liabilities are the creditors for raw material, the wages lag, the outstanding manufacturing overheads and one month of administrative cost.","difficulty":"medium"},"pg":{"q":"2","a":"7","cq":"1-2"},"case":"n-p6-pyq-may2025-fm-c1","sub":"fm","tags":["fm.wc.estimate"]},{"id":"n-p6-pyq-may2025-fm-m5","section":"I","no":5,"marks":2,"type":"MCQ","stem":"What will be the estimated working capital to be submitted by you in the proposal?","options":["₹ 5,33,775","₹ 4,36,725","₹ 5,50,275","₹ 4,50,225"],"group":{"kind":"case","id":"n-p6-pyq-may2025-fm-c1","label":"Case Scenario - I","context":"XYZ Ltd. wants to establish a shoe manufacturing unit. To setup this unit, it needs a loan. XYZ Ltd, approaches a commercial bank for working capital loan.\n\nBank has asked the company to present the proposal for such loan. To prepare such proposal, the company has appointed you and provided some information about the plan.\n\nIt wants to maintain margin of safety of 10% for contingencies. The company want to keep cash balance of ₹ 90,000.\n\nThe product will be sold at gross profit margin of 25% on COGS. Depreciation will be part of cost of production.\n\nStock of raw material will be held at 1.5 months of its consumption, while finished goods inventory will be maintained at one month's requirement. Finished goods will be valued at manufacturing cost.\n\nThe company will sell shoes in domestic as well as in foreign market. Total estimated annual sales will be ₹ 30,00,000. 20% of the sales are foreign sales and there will be a delay of 1/2 month to realise the sales proceed from foreign debtors. All domestic sales will be on credit and allowed a credit period of 2 months.\n\nRaw material consumed will be 25% of estimated sales and the company will get credit period of 2 months of consumption from its supplier of raw material. Wage expenses will be 20% of sales and will be paid in 1/2 month's lag.\n\nOutstanding cash manufacturing overhead expenses at the end of the year will be ₹ 60,000; it will be paid in one month arrear. Estimated total administrative cost (to be paid after one month) will be ₹ 1,80,000. Expected annual sales promotion expenses are ₹ 90,000, which will be paid quarterly in advance. The company want to submit the proposal to bank on cash cost basis.\n\nYou are required to answer the following question 1 to 5:"},"answer":{"kind":"single","value":"A"},"key_raw":"A","label":"MCQ 5","ord":4,"ours":{"approach":"Net working capital is current assets less current liabilities; then add the 10% margin of safety.","difficulty":"medium"},"pg":{"q":"2-3","a":"7","cq":"1-2"},"case":"n-p6-pyq-may2025-fm-c1","sub":"fm","tags":["fm.wc.estimate"]},{"id":"n-p6-pyq-may2025-fm-m6","section":"I","no":6,"marks":2,"type":"MCQ","stem":"A company has net worth of ₹ 5,00,000. Its debt to equity ratio is 2. Interest on debt is 10%. The company earns an operating profit of ₹ 4,00,000. Tax rate is 30%.\n\nWhat will be the financial leverage of the company?","options":["1.11","1.33","1.43","1.90"],"answer":{"kind":"single","value":"B"},"key_raw":"B","label":"MCQ 6","ord":5,"ours":{"approach":"Debt is twice the net worth; financial leverage is EBIT ÷ (EBIT – interest), and tax does not enter it.","difficulty":"easy"},"pg":{"q":"3","a":"7"},"sub":"fm","tags":["fm.lev.leverage"]},{"id":"n-p6-pyq-may2025-fm-m7","section":"I","no":7,"marks":2,"type":"MCQ","stem":"T Ltd. is looking for a capital project in order to replace its existing old machine. It got two proposals to consider; details of which are given below:\n\n| | Proposal X | Proposal Y |\n|---|---:|---:|\n| Initial investment | ₹ 6,50,000 | ₹ 7,80,000 |\n| Estimated useful life | 5 Years | 3 Years |\n| Annal cash inflows | ₹ 1,90,000 | ₹ 3,50,000 |\n| Cost of capital | 10% | 10% |\n\n| Year | 1 | 2 | 3 | 4 | 5 |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n| PVIF$_{0.10,\\, t}$ | 0909 | 0.826 | 0.751 | 0.683 | 0.621 |\n| PVIFA$_{0.10,\\, t}$ | 0.909 | 1.736 | 2.487 | 3.170 | 3.791 |\n\nWhat will be Equivalent Annual NPV for Proposal X and Proposal Y?","options":["₹ 70,290.00, ₹ 90,450.00","₹ 18,541.28, ₹ 36,369.12","₹ 1,90,000.00, ₹ 3,50,000.00","₹ 77,326.73, ₹ 99,504.95"],"answer":{"kind":"single","value":"B"},"key_raw":"B","label":"MCQ 7","ord":6,"ours":{"approach":"Find each proposal's NPV with the annuity factor for its own life, then divide that NPV by the same annuity factor.","difficulty":"medium"},"pg":{"q":"3","a":"7"},"note":"ICAI prints \"Annal cash inflows\" and a PVIF of \"0909\" for year 1; both kept as printed.","sub":"fm","tags":["fm.capbud.npv"]},{"id":"n-p6-pyq-may2025-fm-m8","section":"I","no":8,"marks":1,"type":"MCQ","stem":"Q Ltd. is planning to pay dividend of ₹ 2 per share in next year. Growth rate of company is 8% p.a. Current market price per share is ₹ 51. Flotation cost is ₹ 1 per share.\n\nWhat will be cost of equity?","options":["12.23%","11.92%","12.00%","12.32%"],"answer":{"kind":"single","value":"C"},"key_raw":"C","label":"MCQ 8","ord":7,"ours":{"approach":"Dividend growth model on the net proceeds: D1 ÷ (P0 – flotation cost) + g.","difficulty":"easy"},"pg":{"q":"3-4","a":"7"},"sub":"fm","tags":["fm.coc.equity"]},{"id":"n-p6-pyq-may2025-fm-q1a","section":"II","no":1,"marks":5,"type":"DESC","stem":"The following information is available for S Ltd. for the year ended 31st March, 2025:\n\n| | |\n|---|---:|\n| Raw Material consumed | 20% of COGS |\n| Raw Material Inventory turnover ratio | 4.00 |\n| Finished Goods Inventory holding period | 0.75 month |\n| Gross profit (based on COGS) | 12.50% |\n| Debtor collection period (all sales are credit sales) | 3 months |\n| Proprietary ratio | 0.3125 |\n| Fixed Assets turnover ratio (based on sales) | 3.00 |\n| Fixed Assets to Total Assets | 40% |\n\nYou are required to prepare a Balance Sheet as on 31st March, 2025 in the following format:\n\n| Liabilities | ₹ | Assets | ₹ |\n|---|---:|---|---:|\n| Shareholders’ Fund (assume no Preference Shares) | | Fixed Assets | 12,00,000 |\n| Long-term Debt | 15,00,000 | Stock of Raw Material | |\n| Current Liabilities | | Stock of Finished Goods | |\n| | | Debtors | |\n| | | Cash | |\n| Total | | Total | |","label":"Q1(a)","part":"(a)","ord":8,"ans":"**Workings:**\n\n**(i) Calculation of Sales, Cost of Goods Sold and Gross Profit**\n\nFixed Assets Turnover = $\\dfrac{\\text{Sales}}{\\text{Fixed Assets}}$\n\n3 = $\\dfrac{\\text{Sales}}{12{,}00{,}000}$\n\nSo, Sales = 12,00,000 x 3 = ₹ 36,00,000\n\nAnd Cost of Goods Sold = 36,00,000 x $\\dfrac{100}{(100 + 12.5)}$ = ₹ 32,00,000\n\nTherefore, Gross Profit = 32,00,000 x 12.5% = ₹ 4,00,000\n\n**(ii) Calculation of Stock of Raw Materials**\n\nRaw Material Consumed = COGS x 20%\n\n= 32,00,000 x 20%\n\n= ₹ 6,40,000\n\nSo, stock of raw material = $\\dfrac{6{,}40{,}000}{4}$ = ₹ 1,60,000\n\n**(iii) Calculation of Debtors**\n\nDebtor Collection Period = $\\dfrac{12}{\\text{Receivable Turnover Ratio}}$ = 3 months\n\nOr Debtors = 36,00,000 X $\\dfrac{3}{12}$\n\n= ₹ 9,00,000\n\n**(iv) Calculation of Total Assets**\n\nTotal Asset = $\\dfrac{\\text{Fixed Assets}}{40\\%}$\n\n= 12,00,000 x $\\dfrac{100}{40}$ = ₹ 30,00,000\n\nSo Total Liabilities including shareholder’s fund = ₹ 30,00,000\n\n**(v) Calculation of Stock of Finished Goods**\n\nStock of Finished Goods = 32,00,000 x $\\dfrac{0.75}{12}$ = ₹ 2,00,000\n\n**(vi) Calculation of Current Assets and Fixed Assets**\n\nCurrent Assets = Total Assets – Fixed Assets\n\n= 30,00,000 – 12,00,000 = ₹ 18,00,000\n\nAnd Cash Balance = Current Assets – (Raw Material + Finished Goods + Debtors)\n\n= 18,00,000 – (1,60,000 + 2,00,000 + 9,00,000)\n\n= ₹ 5,40,000\n\n**(vii) Calculation of Shareholder’s Fund**\n\nProprietary Ratio = $\\dfrac{\\text{Shareholders Fund}}{\\text{Total Assets}}$\n\n0.3125 = $\\dfrac{\\text{Shareholders Fund}}{30{,}00{,}000}$\n\nShareholders Fund = ₹ 9,37,500\n\n**Balance Sheet as on 31st March 2025**\n\n| Liabilities | (₹) | Assets | (₹) |\n|---|---:|---|---:|\n| Shareholders’ Fund | 9,37,500 | Fixed Assets | 12,00,000 |\n| Long-term Debt | 15,00,000 | Stock of Raw Material | 1,60,000 |\n| Current Liabilities (Balancing Figure) | 5,62,500 | Stock of Finished Goods | 2,00,000 |\n| | | Debtors | 9,00,000 |\n| | | Cash | 5,40,000 |\n| | **30,00,000** | | **30,00,000** |","ours":{"approach":"Start from the one figure in rupees: fixed assets give sales (turnover 3) and total assets (40%). The gross profit on COGS gives COGS, and every stock and debtor figure follows from it; cash and current liabilities are balancing figures.","difficulty":"medium"},"pg":{"q":"8","a":"9-11"},"sub":"fm","tags":["fm.ratio.prepare","fm.ratio.activity"]},{"id":"n-p6-pyq-may2025-fm-q1b-i","section":"II","no":1,"marks":2,"type":"DESC","stem":"What will be retention ratio if the company wants to maintain its P/E ratio to 12 in current year, given that the expected rate of return for an investor is 20%?","group":{"kind":"case","id":"n-p6-pyq-may2025-fm-c2","label":null,"context":"Y Ltd. produces energy drinks in different flavours. Due to high demand of its product, the rate of return on its earnings is 25%. Currently, the company retains 60% of its earnings and distributes the rest. The current P/E ratio is 8 and earnings per share is ₹ 10.\n\nAccording to Gordon's Model:","answer":"The question can be solved with the help of Justified P/E Ratio linked to Gordon Growth Model."},"label":"Q1(b)(i)","part":"(b)(i)","ord":9,"ans":"**Calculation of Retention Ratio**\n\nP/E = $\\dfrac{1-b}{K_e-br}$\n\n12 = $\\dfrac{1-b}{0.20-0.25b}$\n\nOr 2.4 – 3b =1-b\n\nOr 2.4 – 1 = 3b – b\n\nOr 2b = 1.4\n\nOr b = 0.70 or 70%\n\nSo, Retention Ratio is 70%, if the company wants to maintain its P/E ratio to 12 in the current year.","ours":{"approach":"Under Gordon's model the justified P/E is (1 – b) ÷ (Ke – br); put in the target P/E, Ke and r and solve for b.","difficulty":"medium"},"pg":{"q":"9","a":"11-12","ca":"11"},"case":"n-p6-pyq-may2025-fm-c2","sub":"fm","tags":["fm.div.models"]},{"id":"n-p6-pyq-may2025-fm-q1b-ii","section":"II","no":1,"marks":1,"type":"DESC","stem":"What will be the expected price per share after one year if Y Ltd. achieves above-mentioned targeted P/E ratio?","group":{"kind":"case","id":"n-p6-pyq-may2025-fm-c2","label":null,"context":"Y Ltd. produces energy drinks in different flavours. Due to high demand of its product, the rate of return on its earnings is 25%. Currently, the company retains 60% of its earnings and distributes the rest. The current P/E ratio is 8 and earnings per share is ₹ 10.\n\nAccording to Gordon's Model:","answer":"The question can be solved with the help of Justified P/E Ratio linked to Gordon Growth Model."},"label":"Q1(b)(ii)","part":"(b)(ii)","ord":10,"ans":"**Calculation of Expected Price Per Share**\n\nP/E = $\\dfrac{\\text{Market Price Share}}{\\text{Earning Per Share}}$\n\nP/E Ratio is 12 and EPS is ₹ 10\n\nSo, Market Price Share = P/E Ratio x Earning Per Share\n\n= 12 x 10 = ₹ 120\n\nRetention Ratio is 70%, when P/E ratio is 12, Return on Earnings is 25%\n\nSo, New growth rate = 0.70 X 0.25 = 0.175 = 17.5%\n\nExpected price after one year = ₹ 120 X (1+17.5%) = ₹ 141\n\nTherefore, the expected price per share after one year is ₹ 141 if Y Ltd. achieves the targeted P/E ratio of 12.","ours":{"approach":"Today's price is the target P/E times EPS; it grows at g = b × r, with the retention ratio found in (i).","difficulty":"easy"},"pg":{"q":"9","a":"12","ca":"11"},"case":"n-p6-pyq-may2025-fm-c2","sub":"fm","tags":["fm.div.models"]},{"id":"n-p6-pyq-may2025-fm-q1b-iii","section":"II","no":1,"marks":2,"type":"DESC","stem":"Will there be any change in retention ratio if the company wants to maintain its P/E ratio to 10 in current year, given that the expected rate of return for an investor is 17.50%?","group":{"kind":"case","id":"n-p6-pyq-may2025-fm-c2","label":null,"context":"Y Ltd. produces energy drinks in different flavours. Due to high demand of its product, the rate of return on its earnings is 25%. Currently, the company retains 60% of its earnings and distributes the rest. The current P/E ratio is 8 and earnings per share is ₹ 10.\n\nAccording to Gordon's Model:","answer":"The question can be solved with the help of Justified P/E Ratio linked to Gordon Growth Model."},"label":"Q1(b)(iii)","part":"(b)(iii)","ord":11,"ans":"**Calculation of change in Retention Ratio**\n\nP/E = $\\dfrac{1-b}{K_e-br}$\n\n10 = $\\dfrac{1-b}{0.175 - 0.25b}$\n\n1.75 – 2.5b = 1-b\n\n1.75 – 1 = 2.5b – b\n\n1.5 b = 0.75\n\nb = $\\dfrac{0.75}{1.5}$ = 0.5 or 50%\n\nSo, Retention Ratio is 50%, if the company wants to maintain its P/E ratio to 10 in the current year.","ours":{"difficulty":"medium"},"pg":{"q":"9","a":"12","ca":"11"},"case":"n-p6-pyq-may2025-fm-c2","sub":"fm","tags":["fm.div.models"]},{"id":"n-p6-pyq-may2025-fm-q1c-i","section":"II","no":1,"marks":2,"type":"DESC","stem":"Prepare Income Statement for the year ended 31st March, 2025.","group":{"kind":"case","id":"n-p6-pyq-may2025-fm-c3","label":null,"context":"Following information relates to A Ltd. for the year ended 31st March, 2025:\n\n| | |\n|---|---:|\n| Profit volume ratio | 24% |\n| Operating leverage | 2.00 |\n| Financial leverage | 1.50 |\n| Interest Expenses | ₹ 12,000 |\n| Tax rate | 30% |\n| Number of Equity Shares | 1,000 |\n\nYou are required to:","answer":"**Workings:**\n\n(a) Financial Leverage = $\\dfrac{\\text{EBIT}}{\\text{EBT i.e EBIT – Interest}}$\n\nSo, 1.5 = $\\dfrac{\\text{EBIT}}{\\text{EBIT– 12,000}}$\n\nOr, 1.5 (EBIT – 12,000) = EBIT\n\nOr, 0.5 EBIT = 18,000\n\nOr, EBIT = ₹ 36,000\n\n(b) Operating Leverage = $\\dfrac{\\text{Contribution}}{\\text{EBIT}}$ Or, 2 = $\\dfrac{\\text{Contribution}}{\\text{₹ 36,000}}$\n\nOr, Contribution = ₹ 72,000\n\nSales = $\\dfrac{\\text{Contribution}}{\\text{P/V Ratio}}$\n\n= $\\dfrac{\\text{₹ 72,000}}{0.24}$ = ₹ 3,00,000\n\n(c) Fixed Cost = Contribution – EBIT\n\n= 72,000 – 36,000\n\nOr, Fixed cost = ₹ 36,000"},"label":"Q1(c)(i)","part":"(c)(i)","ord":12,"ans":"**Income Statements for the year ended 31st March 2025**\n\n| Particulars | A Ltd. (₹) |\n|---|---:|\n| Sales | 3,00,000 |\n| Less: Variable cost (76%) | 2,28,000 |\n| Contribution | 72,000 |\n| Less: Fixed Cost | 36,000 |\n| Earnings before interest and tax (EBIT) | 36,000 |\n| Less: Interest | 12,000 |\n| Earnings before tax (EBT) | 24,000 |\n| Less: Tax @ 30% | 7,200 |\n| Earnings after tax (EAT) | 16,800 |","ours":{"approach":"Work upwards from the interest: financial leverage gives EBIT, operating leverage gives contribution, and the P/V ratio turns contribution into sales.","difficulty":"medium"},"pg":{"q":"9","a":"13"},"case":"n-p6-pyq-may2025-fm-c3","sub":"fm","tags":["fm.lev.leverage"]},{"id":"n-p6-pyq-may2025-fm-q1c-ii","section":"II","no":1,"marks":1,"type":"DESC","stem":"Calculate EPS.","group":{"kind":"case","id":"n-p6-pyq-may2025-fm-c3","label":null,"context":"Following information relates to A Ltd. for the year ended 31st March, 2025:\n\n| | |\n|---|---:|\n| Profit volume ratio | 24% |\n| Operating leverage | 2.00 |\n| Financial leverage | 1.50 |\n| Interest Expenses | ₹ 12,000 |\n| Tax rate | 30% |\n| Number of Equity Shares | 1,000 |\n\nYou are required to:","answer":"**Workings:**\n\n(a) Financial Leverage = $\\dfrac{\\text{EBIT}}{\\text{EBT i.e EBIT – Interest}}$\n\nSo, 1.5 = $\\dfrac{\\text{EBIT}}{\\text{EBIT– 12,000}}$\n\nOr, 1.5 (EBIT – 12,000) = EBIT\n\nOr, 0.5 EBIT = 18,000\n\nOr, EBIT = ₹ 36,000\n\n(b) Operating Leverage = $\\dfrac{\\text{Contribution}}{\\text{EBIT}}$ Or, 2 = $\\dfrac{\\text{Contribution}}{\\text{₹ 36,000}}$\n\nOr, Contribution = ₹ 72,000\n\nSales = $\\dfrac{\\text{Contribution}}{\\text{P/V Ratio}}$\n\n= $\\dfrac{\\text{₹ 72,000}}{0.24}$ = ₹ 3,00,000\n\n(c) Fixed Cost = Contribution – EBIT\n\n= 72,000 – 36,000\n\nOr, Fixed cost = ₹ 36,000"},"label":"Q1(c)(ii)","part":"(c)(ii)","ord":13,"ans":"**Calculation of EPS**\n\nEPS = $\\dfrac{\\text{Profit after tax}}{\\text{No. of Equity Shares}} = \\dfrac{16{,}800}{1{,}000}$ = ₹16.8","ours":{"difficulty":"easy"},"pg":{"q":"9","a":"14","ca":"13"},"case":"n-p6-pyq-may2025-fm-c3","sub":"fm","tags":["fm.lev.leverage","fm.ratio.profit"]},{"id":"n-p6-pyq-may2025-fm-q1c-iii","section":"II","no":1,"marks":2,"type":"DESC","stem":"Calculate percentage change in earnings per share, if sales increase by 5%.","group":{"kind":"case","id":"n-p6-pyq-may2025-fm-c3","label":null,"context":"Following information relates to A Ltd. for the year ended 31st March, 2025:\n\n| | |\n|---|---:|\n| Profit volume ratio | 24% |\n| Operating leverage | 2.00 |\n| Financial leverage | 1.50 |\n| Interest Expenses | ₹ 12,000 |\n| Tax rate | 30% |\n| Number of Equity Shares | 1,000 |\n\nYou are required to:","answer":"**Workings:**\n\n(a) Financial Leverage = $\\dfrac{\\text{EBIT}}{\\text{EBT i.e EBIT – Interest}}$\n\nSo, 1.5 = $\\dfrac{\\text{EBIT}}{\\text{EBIT– 12,000}}$\n\nOr, 1.5 (EBIT – 12,000) = EBIT\n\nOr, 0.5 EBIT = 18,000\n\nOr, EBIT = ₹ 36,000\n\n(b) Operating Leverage = $\\dfrac{\\text{Contribution}}{\\text{EBIT}}$ Or, 2 = $\\dfrac{\\text{Contribution}}{\\text{₹ 36,000}}$\n\nOr, Contribution = ₹ 72,000\n\nSales = $\\dfrac{\\text{Contribution}}{\\text{P/V Ratio}}$\n\n= $\\dfrac{\\text{₹ 72,000}}{0.24}$ = ₹ 3,00,000\n\n(c) Fixed Cost = Contribution – EBIT\n\n= 72,000 – 36,000\n\nOr, Fixed cost = ₹ 36,000"},"label":"Q1(c)(iii)","part":"(c)(iii)","ord":14,"ans":"**Calculation of % change in EPS if Sales increase by 5%**\n\nDegree of Combined Leverage (DCL) = Operating Leverage x Financial Leverage\n\n= 2 x 1.5 = 3\n\n**Alternatively, solution can be presented in following way:**\n\nDCL = $\\dfrac{\\text{Contribution}}{\\text{EBT}} = \\dfrac{72{,}000}{24{,}000}$ =3\n\nSo, $\\dfrac{\\text{\\% change in EPS}}{\\text{\\% change in Sales}}$ = 3\n\nOr % change in EPS = DCL x % change in Sales\n\n= 3 x 0.05 = 0.15 or 15%","ours":{"approach":"Combined leverage (operating × financial) is the percentage change in EPS for a 1% change in sales.","difficulty":"easy"},"pg":{"q":"9","a":"14","ca":"13"},"case":"n-p6-pyq-may2025-fm-c3","sub":"fm","tags":["fm.lev.leverage"]},{"id":"n-p6-pyq-may2025-fm-q2a-i","section":"II","no":2,"marks":1,"type":"DESC","stem":"Cost of Equity.","group":{"kind":"case","id":"n-p6-pyq-may2025-fm-c4","label":null,"context":"Capital structure of B Ltd. for the year ended 31st March, 2025 are as follows:\n\n| Particulars | Amount (₹) |\n|---|---:|\n| Equity share capital @₹10 each | 14,00,000 |\n| 10% Preference share capital @₹1,000 each | 10,00,000 |\n| Debenture @ ₹100 each | 9,60,000 |\n| Bank Loan | 6,40,000 |\n\n- Risk-free rate of return is 14%, Market rate of return is 19% and beta of company is 1.20.\n- 10% Preference shares are redeemable at ₹ 1,065.40 after 3 years.\n- Interest on bank loan is 1.30 times of interest on debentures.\n- Debentures are redeemable at par after 5 years. Floatation cost is ₹ 4 per debenture.\n- Tax rate is 30%.\n- Cost of capital is 14%.\n\nYou are required to calculate the following:\n\nPV factors @ 10% and 14%\n\n| Year | 1 | 2 | 3 | 4 |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n| PVIF$_{0.10, t}$ | 0.909 | 0.826 | 0.751 | 0.683 |\n| PVIF$_{0.14, t}$ | 0.877 | 0.769 | 0.675 | 0.592 |"},"label":"Q2(a)(i)","part":"(a)(i)","ord":15,"ans":"**Calculation of Cost of Equity**\n\nKe = Rf + β (Rm – Rf) = 14% + 1.2 (19% - 14%) = 20%","ours":{"difficulty":"easy"},"pg":{"q":"15","a":"15","cq":"14-15"},"case":"n-p6-pyq-may2025-fm-c4","sub":"fm","tags":["fm.coc.equity"]},{"id":"n-p6-pyq-may2025-fm-q2a-ii","section":"II","no":2,"marks":2,"type":"DESC","stem":"Cost of preference share using YTM method.","group":{"kind":"case","id":"n-p6-pyq-may2025-fm-c4","label":null,"context":"Capital structure of B Ltd. for the year ended 31st March, 2025 are as follows:\n\n| Particulars | Amount (₹) |\n|---|---:|\n| Equity share capital @₹10 each | 14,00,000 |\n| 10% Preference share capital @₹1,000 each | 10,00,000 |\n| Debenture @ ₹100 each | 9,60,000 |\n| Bank Loan | 6,40,000 |\n\n- Risk-free rate of return is 14%, Market rate of return is 19% and beta of company is 1.20.\n- 10% Preference shares are redeemable at ₹ 1,065.40 after 3 years.\n- Interest on bank loan is 1.30 times of interest on debentures.\n- Debentures are redeemable at par after 5 years. Floatation cost is ₹ 4 per debenture.\n- Tax rate is 30%.\n- Cost of capital is 14%.\n\nYou are required to calculate the following:\n\nPV factors @ 10% and 14%\n\n| Year | 1 | 2 | 3 | 4 |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n| PVIF$_{0.10, t}$ | 0.909 | 0.826 | 0.751 | 0.683 |\n| PVIF$_{0.14, t}$ | 0.877 | 0.769 | 0.675 | 0.592 |"},"label":"Q2(a)(ii)","part":"(a)(ii)","ord":16,"ans":"**Calculation of Cost of Preference Shares:**\n\nCurrent Market Price P0\n\n= Preference Dividend (PD) X PVAF (r,14) + Redeemable Value (RV) X PVIF (r,14)\n\nOr 1000 = 100 x PVAF (r,14) + 1065.4 x (r,14)\n\nCalculation of NPV at discount rate of 10% and 14%\n\n| Year | Cash Flows (₹) | PVF @ 10% | PV (₹) | PVF @ 14% | PV (₹) |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n| 0 | (1000) | 1 | (1000) | 1 | (1000) |\n| 1-3 | 100 | 2.487 | 248.70 | 2.322 | 232.20 |\n| 3 | 1065.4 | 0.751 | 800.12 | 0.675 | 719.14 |\n| | | | **+48.82** | | **-48.66** |\n\nIRR = 10% + $\\dfrac{48.82}{48.82-(-48.66)}$ x (14% - 10%)\n\n= 10% + $\\dfrac{48.82}{97.48}$ x 4%\n\n= 10% + 2% = 12%","ours":{"approach":"The preference share is a 3-year bond: price 1,000, dividend 100 a year and redemption at 1,065.40. Find the NPV at two rates either side and interpolate for the YTM.","difficulty":"medium"},"pg":{"q":"15","a":"15-16","cq":"14-15"},"case":"n-p6-pyq-may2025-fm-c4","sub":"fm","tags":["fm.coc.debt"]},{"id":"n-p6-pyq-may2025-fm-q2a-iii","section":"II","no":2,"marks":3,"type":"DESC","stem":"Post-tax cost of debenture using approximation method.","group":{"kind":"case","id":"n-p6-pyq-may2025-fm-c4","label":null,"context":"Capital structure of B Ltd. for the year ended 31st March, 2025 are as follows:\n\n| Particulars | Amount (₹) |\n|---|---:|\n| Equity share capital @₹10 each | 14,00,000 |\n| 10% Preference share capital @₹1,000 each | 10,00,000 |\n| Debenture @ ₹100 each | 9,60,000 |\n| Bank Loan | 6,40,000 |\n\n- Risk-free rate of return is 14%, Market rate of return is 19% and beta of company is 1.20.\n- 10% Preference shares are redeemable at ₹ 1,065.40 after 3 years.\n- Interest on bank loan is 1.30 times of interest on debentures.\n- Debentures are redeemable at par after 5 years. Floatation cost is ₹ 4 per debenture.\n- Tax rate is 30%.\n- Cost of capital is 14%.\n\nYou are required to calculate the following:\n\nPV factors @ 10% and 14%\n\n| Year | 1 | 2 | 3 | 4 |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n| PVIF$_{0.10, t}$ | 0.909 | 0.826 | 0.751 | 0.683 |\n| PVIF$_{0.14, t}$ | 0.877 | 0.769 | 0.675 | 0.592 |"},"label":"Q2(a)(iii)","part":"(a)(iii)","ord":17,"ans":"**Calculation of Post-tax Cost of Convertible Debentures:**\n\n**Working Note:**\n\nCalculation of Interest rate on Bank term Loan & Debentures\n\nLet the rate of interest on debentures be ‘X’\n\nTherefore, Rate of Interest on bank loan = 1.3X x (0.7) = 0.91X\n\nKd (Debentures) = $\\dfrac{I(1-t)+\\dfrac{(RV-NP)}{n}}{\\dfrac{(RV+NP)}{2}}$\n\n= $\\dfrac{100X(1-0.3)+\\dfrac{(100-96)}{5}}{\\dfrac{(100+96)}{2}}$\n\n= $\\dfrac{70X+0.8}{98}$\n\n| Sources of capital | Amount of capital | Weights (W) | Cost (K) | W x K |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n| Equity share capital | 14,00,000 | 0.35 | 0.20 | 0.07 |\n| Preference share capital | 10,00,000 | 0.25 | 0.12 | 0.03 |\n| Debentures | 9,60,000 | 0.24 | $\\dfrac{70X+ 0.8}{98}$ | $\\dfrac{16.8X + 0.192}{98}$ |\n| Bank Loan | 6,40,000 | 0.16 | 0.91X | 0.16 x 0.91X |\n| | 40,00,000 | 1.00 | | 0.14 |\n\n0.14 = 0.07 + 0.03 + $\\dfrac{16.8X+0.192}{98}$ + 0.16x(0.91X)\n\n0.04 x 98 = 16.8 X +0.192 + 14.2688 X\n\n3.728 = 31.0688X\n\nX = 0.1199 or 12 %\n\nSo, interest on debentures = 12 %\n\nKd (Debentures) = $\\dfrac{I(1-t)+\\dfrac{(RV-NP)}{n}}{\\dfrac{(RV+NP)}{2}}$\n\n= $\\dfrac{12(1-0.3)+\\dfrac{(100-96)}{5}}{\\dfrac{(100+96)}{2}}$\n\n= $\\dfrac{8.4+0.8}{98}$ = 9.387% or 9.39%","ours":{"approach":"The debenture rate is the unknown: write the post-tax cost of the debenture and of the bank loan in terms of it, put them in the WACC with the costs from (i) and (ii), and solve against the given 14%.","difficulty":"hard"},"pg":{"q":"15","a":"16-17","cq":"14-15"},"case":"n-p6-pyq-may2025-fm-c4","sub":"fm","tags":["fm.coc.debt","fm.coc.wacc"]},{"id":"n-p6-pyq-may2025-fm-q2a-iv","section":"II","no":2,"marks":2,"type":"DESC","stem":"Interest rate of bank loan.","group":{"kind":"case","id":"n-p6-pyq-may2025-fm-c4","label":null,"context":"Capital structure of B Ltd. for the year ended 31st March, 2025 are as follows:\n\n| Particulars | Amount (₹) |\n|---|---:|\n| Equity share capital @₹10 each | 14,00,000 |\n| 10% Preference share capital @₹1,000 each | 10,00,000 |\n| Debenture @ ₹100 each | 9,60,000 |\n| Bank Loan | 6,40,000 |\n\n- Risk-free rate of return is 14%, Market rate of return is 19% and beta of company is 1.20.\n- 10% Preference shares are redeemable at ₹ 1,065.40 after 3 years.\n- Interest on bank loan is 1.30 times of interest on debentures.\n- Debentures are redeemable at par after 5 years. Floatation cost is ₹ 4 per debenture.\n- Tax rate is 30%.\n- Cost of capital is 14%.\n\nYou are required to calculate the following:\n\nPV factors @ 10% and 14%\n\n| Year | 1 | 2 | 3 | 4 |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n| PVIF$_{0.10, t}$ | 0.909 | 0.826 | 0.751 | 0.683 |\n| PVIF$_{0.14, t}$ | 0.877 | 0.769 | 0.675 | 0.592 |"},"label":"Q2(a)(iv)","part":"(a)(iv)","ord":18,"ans":"**Interest Rate of bank loan**\n\n= 12 x 1.3 = 15.60%","ours":{"difficulty":"medium"},"pg":{"q":"15","a":"17","cq":"14-15"},"case":"n-p6-pyq-may2025-fm-c4","sub":"fm","tags":["fm.coc.wacc","fm.coc.debt"]},{"id":"n-p6-pyq-may2025-fm-q2b","section":"II","no":2,"marks":2,"type":"DESC","stem":"Following details are related to H Ltd.:\n\n| | |\n|---|---:|\n| EPS | ₹ 3.00 |\n| Return on investment | 20% |\n| Cost of equity | 15% |\n\nAs per Walter's Model, what would be the maximum and minimum price of share?","label":"Q2(b)","part":"(b)","ord":19,"ans":"In this question, since r>Ke, as per the Walter’s Model, the price will be maximum when the company retains all the earnings. Detailed calculations are as follows:\n\n**(i) Maximum Price when company retains all the earnings i.e. D = 0**\n\nPo = $\\dfrac{D+\\dfrac{r}{Ke}(E-D)}{K_e}$\n\n= $\\dfrac{0+\\dfrac{0.20}{0.15}(3-0)}{0.15} = \\dfrac{(1.333)\\text{x } 3}{0.15} = \\dfrac{4}{0.15}$\n\n= ₹ 26.67\n\n**(ii) Minimum Price is when company distributes all its earnings**\n\nPo = $\\dfrac{3+\\dfrac{0.20}{0.15}(3-3)}{0.15} = \\dfrac{3+0}{0.15} = \\dfrac{3}{0.15}$ = ₹ 20","ours":{"approach":"Compare r with Ke first: when r > Ke, Walter's price is highest at zero payout and lowest at full payout.","difficulty":"easy"},"pg":{"q":"15","a":"17-18"},"sub":"fm","tags":["fm.div.models"]},{"id":"n-p6-pyq-may2025-fm-q3a-i","section":"II","no":3,"marks":1,"type":"DESC","stem":"Initial investment","group":{"kind":"case","id":"n-p6-pyq-may2025-fm-c5","label":null,"context":"Following data are given for a capital project:\n\n| | |\n|---|---:|\n| Annual interim cash inflows for first two years | ₹ 1,00,000 |\n| Annual interim cash inflows for next two years | ₹ 80,000 |\n| Useful life | 4 Years |\n| Salvage value at end of the project | ₹ 50,000 |\n| Internal rate of return | 12% |\n| Cost of capital | 10% |\n\nYou are required to calculate the following:\n\n| Year | 1 | 2 | 3 | 4 |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n| PVIF$_{0.09, t}$ | 0.917 | 0.842 | 0.772 | 0.708 |\n| PVIF$_{0.10, t}$ | 0.909 | 0.826 | 0.751 | 0.683 |\n| PVIF$_{0.11, t}$ | 0.901 | 0.812 | 0.731 | 0.659 |\n| PVIF$_{0.12, t}$ | 0.893 | 0.797 | 0.712 | 0.636 |\n| PVIF$_{0.10, t}$ | 1.100 | 1.210 | 1.331 | 1.464 |\n| PVIF$_{0.12, t}$ | 1.120 | 1.254 | 1.405 | 1.574 |"},"label":"Q3(a)(i)","part":"(a)(i)","ord":20,"ans":"**Initial Investment**\n\nUsing IRR = 12%, the NPV is zero. So, the initial investment is equal to the present value of inflows at 12%.\n\n| Year | Cash Inflows (₹) | PVF@12% | Present Value (₹) |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|\n| 1 | 1,00,000 | 0.893 | 89,300 |\n| 2 | 1,00,000 | 0.797 | 79,700 |\n| 3 | 80,000 | 0.712 | 56,960 |\n| 4 | 80,000 | 0.636 | 50,880 |\n| 4 | 50,000 (salvage) | 0.636 | 31,800 |\n| | | **Total** | **3,08,640** |\n\nTotal Present Value = ₹ 3,08,640. So, Initial Investment = ₹ 3,08,640.","ours":{"approach":"At the IRR the NPV is zero, so the outlay equals the inflows (salvage included) discounted at the IRR.","difficulty":"easy"},"pg":{"q":"18","a":"19"},"case":"n-p6-pyq-may2025-fm-c5","sub":"fm","tags":["fm.capbud.irr","fm.capbud.npv"]},{"id":"n-p6-pyq-may2025-fm-q3a-ii","section":"II","no":3,"marks":1,"type":"DESC","stem":"Net present value","group":{"kind":"case","id":"n-p6-pyq-may2025-fm-c5","label":null,"context":"Following data are given for a capital project:\n\n| | |\n|---|---:|\n| Annual interim cash inflows for first two years | ₹ 1,00,000 |\n| Annual interim cash inflows for next two years | ₹ 80,000 |\n| Useful life | 4 Years |\n| Salvage value at end of the project | ₹ 50,000 |\n| Internal rate of return | 12% |\n| Cost of capital | 10% |\n\nYou are required to calculate the following:\n\n| Year | 1 | 2 | 3 | 4 |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n| PVIF$_{0.09, t}$ | 0.917 | 0.842 | 0.772 | 0.708 |\n| PVIF$_{0.10, t}$ | 0.909 | 0.826 | 0.751 | 0.683 |\n| PVIF$_{0.11, t}$ | 0.901 | 0.812 | 0.731 | 0.659 |\n| PVIF$_{0.12, t}$ | 0.893 | 0.797 | 0.712 | 0.636 |\n| PVIF$_{0.10, t}$ | 1.100 | 1.210 | 1.331 | 1.464 |\n| PVIF$_{0.12, t}$ | 1.120 | 1.254 | 1.405 | 1.574 |"},"label":"Q3(a)(ii)","part":"(a)(ii)","ord":21,"ans":"**Net Present Value (NPV)**\n\n| Year | Cash Flows (₹) | PVF@10% | Present Value (₹) |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|\n| 0 | (3,08,640) | 1 | (3,08,640) |\n| 1 | 1,00,000 | 0.909 | 90,900 |\n| 2 | 1,00,000 | 0.826 | 82,600 |\n| 3 | 80,000 | 0.751 | 60,080 |\n| 4 | 80,000 | 0.683 | 54,640 |\n| 4 | 50,000 (salvage) | 0.683 | 34,150 |\n| | Sum of PV of Cash Inflows | | 3,22,370 |\n| NPV | (3,22,370-3,08,640) | | **13,730** |","ours":{"difficulty":"easy"},"pg":{"q":"18","a":"19"},"case":"n-p6-pyq-may2025-fm-c5","sub":"fm","tags":["fm.capbud.npv"]},{"id":"n-p6-pyq-may2025-fm-q3a-iii","section":"II","no":3,"marks":1,"type":"DESC","stem":"Profitability Index","group":{"kind":"case","id":"n-p6-pyq-may2025-fm-c5","label":null,"context":"Following data are given for a capital project:\n\n| | |\n|---|---:|\n| Annual interim cash inflows for first two years | ₹ 1,00,000 |\n| Annual interim cash inflows for next two years | ₹ 80,000 |\n| Useful life | 4 Years |\n| Salvage value at end of the project | ₹ 50,000 |\n| Internal rate of return | 12% |\n| Cost of capital | 10% |\n\nYou are required to calculate the following:\n\n| Year | 1 | 2 | 3 | 4 |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n| PVIF$_{0.09, t}$ | 0.917 | 0.842 | 0.772 | 0.708 |\n| PVIF$_{0.10, t}$ | 0.909 | 0.826 | 0.751 | 0.683 |\n| PVIF$_{0.11, t}$ | 0.901 | 0.812 | 0.731 | 0.659 |\n| PVIF$_{0.12, t}$ | 0.893 | 0.797 | 0.712 | 0.636 |\n| PVIF$_{0.10, t}$ | 1.100 | 1.210 | 1.331 | 1.464 |\n| PVIF$_{0.12, t}$ | 1.120 | 1.254 | 1.405 | 1.574 |"},"label":"Q3(a)(iii)","part":"(a)(iii)","ord":22,"ans":"**Profitability Index (PI)**\n\nPI = $\\dfrac{\\text{Sum of PV of Cash Inflows}}{\\text{Initial Cash Outflow}} = \\dfrac{\\text{₹ 3,22,370}}{\\text{₹ 3,08,640}}$ = 1.0445","ours":{"difficulty":"easy"},"pg":{"q":"18","a":"20"},"case":"n-p6-pyq-may2025-fm-c5","sub":"fm","tags":["fm.capbud.npv"]},{"id":"n-p6-pyq-may2025-fm-q3a-iv","section":"II","no":3,"marks":1,"type":"DESC","stem":"Discounted payback period","group":{"kind":"case","id":"n-p6-pyq-may2025-fm-c5","label":null,"context":"Following data are given for a capital project:\n\n| | |\n|---|---:|\n| Annual interim cash inflows for first two years | ₹ 1,00,000 |\n| Annual interim cash inflows for next two years | ₹ 80,000 |\n| Useful life | 4 Years |\n| Salvage value at end of the project | ₹ 50,000 |\n| Internal rate of return | 12% |\n| Cost of capital | 10% |\n\nYou are required to calculate the following:\n\n| Year | 1 | 2 | 3 | 4 |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n| PVIF$_{0.09, t}$ | 0.917 | 0.842 | 0.772 | 0.708 |\n| PVIF$_{0.10, t}$ | 0.909 | 0.826 | 0.751 | 0.683 |\n| PVIF$_{0.11, t}$ | 0.901 | 0.812 | 0.731 | 0.659 |\n| PVIF$_{0.12, t}$ | 0.893 | 0.797 | 0.712 | 0.636 |\n| PVIF$_{0.10, t}$ | 1.100 | 1.210 | 1.331 | 1.464 |\n| PVIF$_{0.12, t}$ | 1.120 | 1.254 | 1.405 | 1.574 |"},"label":"Q3(a)(iv)","part":"(a)(iv)","ord":23,"ans":"**Discounted Payback Period:**\n\n| Year | Cash Flows (₹) | PVF@10% | PV (₹) | Cumulative PV (₹) |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n| 1 | 1,00,000 | 0.909 | 90,900 | 90,900 |\n| 2 | 1,00,000 | 0.826 | 82,600 | 1,73,500 |\n| 3 | 80,000 | 0.751 | 60,080 | 2,33,580 |\n| 4 | 1,30,000 (including salvage) | 0.683 | 88,790 | 3,22,370 |\n\nSo, discounted payback = 3 years + $\\dfrac{\\text{₹ 3,08,640 – ₹ 2,33,580}}{\\text{₹ 88,790}}$ = 3.845 years OR 3.85 years OR 3 years 10 months 4 days approx.","ours":{"difficulty":"easy"},"pg":{"q":"18","a":"20"},"case":"n-p6-pyq-may2025-fm-c5","sub":"fm","tags":["fm.capbud.payback"]},{"id":"n-p6-pyq-may2025-fm-q3a-v","section":"II","no":3,"marks":2,"type":"DESC","stem":"MIRR","group":{"kind":"case","id":"n-p6-pyq-may2025-fm-c5","label":null,"context":"Following data are given for a capital project:\n\n| | |\n|---|---:|\n| Annual interim cash inflows for first two years | ₹ 1,00,000 |\n| Annual interim cash inflows for next two years | ₹ 80,000 |\n| Useful life | 4 Years |\n| Salvage value at end of the project | ₹ 50,000 |\n| Internal rate of return | 12% |\n| Cost of capital | 10% |\n\nYou are required to calculate the following:\n\n| Year | 1 | 2 | 3 | 4 |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n| PVIF$_{0.09, t}$ | 0.917 | 0.842 | 0.772 | 0.708 |\n| PVIF$_{0.10, t}$ | 0.909 | 0.826 | 0.751 | 0.683 |\n| PVIF$_{0.11, t}$ | 0.901 | 0.812 | 0.731 | 0.659 |\n| PVIF$_{0.12, t}$ | 0.893 | 0.797 | 0.712 | 0.636 |\n| PVIF$_{0.10, t}$ | 1.100 | 1.210 | 1.331 | 1.464 |\n| PVIF$_{0.12, t}$ | 1.120 | 1.254 | 1.405 | 1.574 |"},"label":"Q3(a)(v)","part":"(a)(v)","ord":24,"ans":"**Modified Internal Rate of Return (MIRR)**\n\nTerminal value of cash inflows compounded to year 4 using 10%:\n\n| Year | Cash Inflows (₹) | @ 10% reinvestment rate factor | Present Value (₹) |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|\n| 1 | 1,00,000 | 1.331 | 1,33,100 |\n| 2 | 1,00,000 | 1.210 | 1,21,000 |\n| 3 | 80,000 | 1.100 | 88,000 |\n| 4 | 80,000 | 1.000 | 80,000 |\n| 4 | 50,000 (salvage) | 1.000 | 50,000 |\n| | | **Total** | **4,72,100** |\n\nTotal Terminal Value = ₹ 4,72,100\n\nNow, 3,08,640/4,72,100 = 0.6537 or 0.654\n\nIf we see 0.654 in year of present value discount table, the rate which is coming is around 11.20%. So, MIRR is around 11.20%.\n\n**Alternatively, solution can be presented in following way for MIRR:**\n\nTotal Return = 4,72,100/3,08,640 = 1.5296\n\n(1+r)$^4$ = 1.5296\n\nMIRR = (1.5296) $^{(1/4)}$ – 1 = 11.20% (Approx)","ours":{"approach":"Compound every inflow to the end of year 4 at the cost of capital (the reinvestment rate), then find the rate that discounts that terminal value back to the outlay.","difficulty":"medium"},"pg":{"q":"18","a":"20-21"},"case":"n-p6-pyq-may2025-fm-c5","sub":"fm","tags":["fm.capbud.irr"]},{"id":"n-p6-pyq-may2025-fm-q3b","section":"II","no":3,"marks":4,"type":"DESC","stem":"Z Ltd. is an unlevered company. It wants to repurchase its equity shares of ₹ 300 lakhs by issue of 12% debentures of same amount. Current market value of Z Ltd. is ₹ 1,400 lakhs. Its cost of capital is 18%. The company will maintain same level of EBIT in future years. Dividend pay-out ratio is 100%. Company pays tax at a rate of 30%.\n\nAs per Modigliani and Miller approach, due to such change in capital structure, what will be impact on the following?\n\n(i) Market Value of Z Ltd.\n\n(ii) Overall cost of capital\n\n(iii) Cost of equity","label":"Q3(b)","part":"(b)","ord":25,"ans":"Market Value of Equity = $\\dfrac{\\text{Net income (NI) for equity holders}}{K_e}$\n\n₹ 1,400 lakhs = $\\dfrac{\\text{Net income (NI) for equity holders}}{0.18}$\n\nNet Income to equity holders/EAT = ₹ 252 lakhs\n\nTherefore, EBIT = $\\dfrac{\\text{EAT}}{(1-t)}$ = $\\dfrac{252 \\text{ lakhs}}{(1 - 0.3)}$ = ₹ 360 lakhs\n\n**Income Statement**\n\n| | All Equity (₹ In lakhs) | Equity & Debt (₹ In lakhs) |\n|---|---:|---:|\n| EBIT (as calculated above) | 360 | 360 |\n| Interest on ₹ 300 lakhs @ 12% | - | 36 |\n| EBT | 360 | 324 |\n| Tax @ 30% | 108 | 97.2 |\n| Income available to equity holders | 252 | 226.8 |\n\n**(i) Market value of Z Ltd**\n\nMarket value of levered firm = Value of unlevered firm + Tax Advantage\n\n= ₹ 1,400 lakhs + (₹ 300 lakhs x 0.3)\n\n= ₹ 1,490 lakhs\n\nChange in market value of the company = 1,490 lakhs – 1,400 lakhs\n\n= ₹ 90 lakhs\n\nThe impact is that the market value of the company has increased by ₹ 90 lakhs due to replacement of equity with debt.\n\n**(ii) Overall Cost of Capital**\n\nMarket Value of Equity = Market value of levered firm - Equity repurchased\n\n= ₹ 1,490 lakhs – ₹ 300 lakhs = ₹ 1,190 lakhs\n\nCost of Equity (K$_e$) = $\\dfrac{\\text{Net Income to equity holders}}{\\text{Market value of equity}}$ × 100\n\n= $\\dfrac{\\text{₹ 226.8 lakhs}}{\\text{₹ 1190 lakhs}}$ × 100 =19.06% (approx.)\n\nCost of debt (K$_d$) = I (1 - t) = 12 (1 - 0.3) = 8.4%\n\n| Components | Amount (₹ In lakhs) | Cost of Capital % | Weight | WACC (K$_o$) % |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n| Equity | 1,190 | 19.06 | 0.80(approx.) | 15.25 |\n| Debt | 300 | 8.4 | 0.20(approx.) | 1.68 |\n| | 1490 | | 1 | 16.93 |\n\n(**Note:** If we take the weight of Equity as 0.7986 and weight of Debt as 0.2013 then WACC will be 16.91%).\n\nImpact on Market value and Overall cost of capital of Z Ltd.\n\n| Particulars | Before restructure | After restructure | Impact |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|\n| Market value of company | ₹ 1,400 lakhs | ₹ 1,490 Lakhs | Increase due to import of debt |\n| Cost of equity | 18% | 19.06% | Increase |\n| Overall cost of capital | 18% | 16.93% | Decrease |\n\nSo, the impact is that the Overall Cost of Capital or K$_o$ has fallen by 1.07% (18% - 16.93%) due to the benefit of tax relief on debt interest payment.\n\n**(iii) Cost of Equity**\n\nThe impact is that cost of equity has risen by 1.06% (19.06% - 18%) due to the presence of financial risk i.e. introduction of debt in capital structure.\n\n**Note:** Cost of Capital and Cost of equity can also be calculated with the help of following formulas,\n\nCost of Capital (K$_o$) = K$_{eu}$ [1 – (t x L)]\n\nWhere,\n\nK$_{eu}$ = Cost of equity in an unlevered company\n\nt = Tax rate\n\nL = $\\dfrac{\\text{Debt}}{\\text{Debt + Equity}}$\n\nSo, K$_o$ = 0.18 $\\left[1- \\left(0.3 \\times \\dfrac{\\text{₹ 300 lakhs}}{\\text{₹ 1490 lakhs}}\\right)\\right]$ = 0.1691 or 16.91% (approx.)\n\nCost of Equity (K$_e$) = K$_{eu}$ + (K$_{eu}$ – K$_d$) $\\dfrac{\\text{Debt (1-t)}}{\\text{Equity}}$\n\nWhere,\n\nK$_{eu}$ = Cost of equity in an unlevered company\n\nK$_d$ = Cost of debt\n\nt = Tax rate\n\nSo, K$_e$ = 0.18 + $\\left((0.18-0.12) \\times \\dfrac{\\text{₹ 300 lakhs (1-0.3)}}{\\text{₹ 1190 lakhs}}\\right)$\n\n= 0.1906 or 19.06%","ours":{"approach":"With 100% payout, equity value = net income ÷ Ke gives EAT and then EBIT. Under MM with tax the levered value is the unlevered value plus the tax shield (debt × tax rate); Ke and Ko follow from the new values.","difficulty":"hard"},"pg":{"q":"18-19","a":"21-23"},"sub":"fm","tags":["fm.cs.theories","fm.coc.wacc"]},{"id":"n-p6-pyq-may2025-fm-q4a","section":"II","no":4,"marks":4,"type":"DESC","stem":"What is agency cost and what are its types? How can a company minimize agency cost and align the interest of manager and shareholder?","label":"Q4(a)","part":"(a)","ord":26,"ans":"**Meaning of Agency Cost:** Agency Problem is the chances that managers may place personal goals ahead of the goal of owners. Agency Problem leads to Agency Cost. Agency cost is the additional cost borne by the shareholders to monitor the manager and control their behaviour so as to maximize shareholders wealth.\n\n**Types of Agency Cost:** Generally, Agency Costs are of four types (i) monitoring (ii) bonding (iii) opportunity (iv) structuring.\n\nFollowing efforts have been made to address the issues of minimizing agency cost and align the interest of manager and shareholders:\n\n- Managerial compensation is linked to the profit of the company to some extent and also with the long-term objectives of the company.\n- Employee is also designed to address the issue with the underlying assumption that maximisation of the stock price is the objective of the investors.\n- Effecting monitoring can be done.","ours":{"difficulty":"easy"},"pg":{"q":"23","a":"24"},"sub":"fm","tags":["fm.intro.agency"]},{"id":"n-p6-pyq-may2025-fm-q4b","section":"II","no":4,"marks":4,"type":"DESC","stem":"What are key features of bridge financing?","label":"Q4(b)","part":"(b)","ord":27,"ans":"**Key features of Bridge Financing**\n\n(i) Bridge finance refers to loans taken by a company normally from commercial banks for a short period because of pending disbursement of loans sanctioned by financial institutions.\n\n(ii) The bridge loans are repaid/ adjusted out of the term loans as and when disbursed by the concerned institutions.\n\n(iii) Bridge loans are normally secured by hypothecating movable assets, personal guarantees and demand promissory notes.\n\n(iv) Generally, the rate of interest on bridge finance is higher as compared with that on term loans.","ours":{"difficulty":"easy"},"pg":{"q":"23","a":"25"},"sub":"fm","tags":["fm.sources.long"]},{"id":"n-p6-pyq-may2025-fm-q4c","section":"II","no":4,"marks":2,"type":"DESC","stem":"What is hierarchy of financing under 'Pecking Order' theory, and why does it exist?","label":"Q4(c)","part":"(c)","ord":28,"ans":"**Hierarchy of financing Pecking order:**\n\nPecking order theory suggests that managers may use various sources for raising of fund in the following order:\n\n1. Managers first choice is to use internal finance.\n2. In absence of internal finance, they can use secured debt, unsecured debt, hybrid debt etc.\n3. Managers may issue new equity shares as a last option.\n\nThe hierarchy of financing Pecking order exist to Minimize the cost of capital, financial control and avoiding negative market signals.","ours":{"difficulty":"easy"},"pg":{"q":"23","a":"25"},"sub":"fm","tags":["fm.cs.theories"]},{"id":"n-p6-pyq-may2025-fm-q4c-or","section":"II","no":4,"marks":2,"type":"DESC","stem":"\"The total risk of any business is the combination of degree of operating and financial risk\". In the light of the above statement, you are required to consider the first two columns of the given table and give your comments in the 3rd column.\n\nYour comments should depict the total risk profile by using the most appropriate amongst the following three words only:\n\n**Lower, Higher and Moderate**\n\nAlso select the best combination of DOL and DFL from the given table.\n\n| DOL | DFL | Comments |\n|---|---|---|\n| Low | High | |\n| High | Low | |\n| High | High | |","label":"Q4(c)","part":"(c)","ord":29,"ans":"**Combination of DOL and DFL**\n\n| DOL | DFL | Comments |\n|---|---|---|\n| Low | High | Moderate total risk. Best combination. Higher financial risk is balanced by lower total business risk. |\n| High | Low | Moderate total risk. Not a good combination. Lower EBIT due to higher DOL and lower advantage of trading on equity due to low DFL. |\n| High | High | Higher total risk. Very risky combination. |","ours":{"difficulty":"easy"},"pg":{"q":"24","a":"25"},"alt":"n-p6-pyq-may2025-fm-q4c","sub":"fm","tags":["fm.lev.leverage"]}]);
